    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p16-q61-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\dfrac1{2n-1}-\\dfrac1{2n+1}=\\dfrac2{4n^2-1}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac2{4n^2-1}$, $n\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12\\}$.\n\nUse the common denominator $(2n-1)(2n+1)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1{2n-1}-\\frac1{2n+1}&=\\frac{(2n+1)-(2n-1)}{(2n-1)(2n+1)}\\\\\\frac1{2n-1}-\\frac1{2n+1}&=\\frac2{4n^2-1}\\end{align*}$$\nOriginal domain: $n\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12\\}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q61-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p16-q61-b-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Hence, prove that",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p16-q61-b-series",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\frac1{1\\times3}+\\frac1{3\\times5}+\\cdots+\\frac1{(2n-1)(2n+1)}=\\frac n{2n+1}.",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac n{2n+1}$, $n\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}$.\n\nApply part (a) to each term; all interior terms cancel, leaving $\\tfrac12(1-\\tfrac1{2n+1})$.",
          "worked": "From part (a),\n$$\\frac1{(2k-1)(2k+1)}=\\frac12\\left(\\frac1{2k-1}-\\frac1{2k+1}\\right).$$\nFor $n\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}$, let $S_n=\\dfrac1{1\\times3}+\\dfrac1{3\\times5}+\\cdots+\\dfrac1{(2n-1)(2n+1)}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}S_n&=\\frac12\\left[\\left(1-\\frac13\\right)+\\left(\\frac13-\\frac15\\right)+\\cdots+\\left(\\frac1{2n-1}-\\frac1{2n+1}\\right)\\right]\\\\S_n&=\\frac12\\left(1-\\frac1{2n+1}\\right)\\\\S_n&=\\frac12\\cdot\\frac{2n}{2n+1}\\\\S_n&=\\frac n{2n+1}\\end{align*}$$\nThe interior terms cancel in pairs. The domain is $n\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}$ because $n$ counts the terms.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q61-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p16-q61-c-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "To what value does the sum below approach, as more terms are added?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p16-q61-c-series",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\frac1{1\\times3}+\\frac1{3\\times5}+\\frac1{5\\times7}+\\cdots",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac12$.\n\nFor $n\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}$, the sum is $\\dfrac1{2+\\frac{1}{n}}$, which approaches $\\dfrac12$ as $n$ increases.",
          "worked": "For $n\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}$, part (b) gives the sum $S_n$ of the first $n$ terms.\n$$\\begin{align*}S_n&=\\frac n{2n+1}\\\\S_n&=\\frac1{2+\\frac{1}{n}}\\end{align*}$$\nAs more terms are added, $n$ increases and $\\frac{1}{n}$ approaches $0$. Therefore the sum approaches $\\dfrac12$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      }
    ]
